@meta
id: 11-09
title: Semicolon and dot, together
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-repeat, dot-design
par: 33
minutes: 6
vscode: find-and-replace scoped to a selection, carefully
open: legacy-ids.ts

@brief
Two batches of legacy order ids, pipe-separated because the system that
produced them was written in 2011. The importer wants commas.

Turn every `|` into a `,`. There are ten of them, five on each line, and
nothing else in either file changes — the ids keep their hyphens.

You have a key that repeats the last find and a key that repeats the last
change. Neither disturbs the other. Put them next to each other and you
have a loop that walks a line and edits as it goes.

@keys
| `f|` | forward to the next pipe |
| `r,` | replace the character under the cursor with a comma |
| `;` | the same find again |
| `.` | the same change again |
| `0` | column zero, for starting the second line |

@hints
Do the first one the long way: find the pipe, replace it. Then look at
your two repeat keys and notice you need exactly one of each.
--
`;` moves to the next pipe. `.` replaces it. `;.` is the whole loop, two
keys per pipe, and you never re-aim either one.
--
`f|r,` to start, then `;.` four times finishes the first line. `j0` drops
to the second, where `;` still remembers the pipe, so it is `;.` five
times from there.

@solution
    f|r,   ;. ;. ;. ;.   j0   ;. ;. ;. ;. ;.

Once the first edit is made, the rest of the job is one rhythm typed with
two fingers. It does not matter how many pipes there are or how far apart
they sit — you are not aiming, you are pressing `;.` until the line stops
changing. The one thing to keep an eye on is the end of the line: `f`
never crosses onto the next line, so `;` simply stops, and a stray `.`
there would replace a character you did want.

That is why the move to the second line is `j0` and not just `j`. After
the last replacement your cursor is deep in the line, and `f` only looks
forward. Sending it back to column zero re-arms the same find for the
whole of the next line.

`;.` is the first of vim's small self-repeating loops. Chapter 12 gives
you the one that works across a whole file rather than a single line —
`n` in place of `;` — and chapter 21 gives you the one that records
several edits at once. They are all the same idea: make the edit once,
make the movement once, then alternate.

@start legacy-ids.ts
export const LEGACY_BATCH_A = 'ORD-1182|ORD-1183|ORD-1184|ORD-1185|ORD-1186|ORD-1187';
export const LEGACY_BATCH_B = 'ORD-1188|ORD-1189|ORD-1190|ORD-1191|ORD-1192|ORD-1193';

@expected legacy-ids.ts
export const LEGACY_BATCH_A = 'ORD-1182,ORD-1183,ORD-1184,ORD-1185,ORD-1186,ORD-1187';
export const LEGACY_BATCH_B = 'ORD-1188,ORD-1189,ORD-1190,ORD-1191,ORD-1192,ORD-1193';
